console.log(toPaise(19.99), formatINR(1999));

questions.answer:
const toPaise = (rupees) => Math.round(rupees * 100);

const formatINR = (paise) =>
  new Intl.NumberFormat("en-IN", {
    style: "currency",
    currency: "INR",
  }).format(paise / 100);

console.log(toPaise(19.99), formatINR(1999));
// 1999 ₹19.99

Why Math.round and not Math.floor: 19.99 * 100 is 1998.9999999999998 in binary floating point. Math.floor would give 1998 — one paisa short. Math.round lands on 1999. All business maths then happens on integers, and Intl handles the symbol, grouping and decimals for display only.

questions.starter_code:
const randomInt = (min, max) => {
  // your code
};

console.log(Array.from({ length: 6 }, () => randomInt(1, 6)));

questions.answer:
const randomInt = (min, max) =>
  Math.floor(Math.random() * (max - min + 1)) + min;

console.log(Array.from({ length: 6 }, () => randomInt(1, 6)));
// e.g. [ 3, 6, 1, 4, 2, 5 ]

Math.random() returns 0 ≤ x < 1, never 1. For 1..6 the span is 6 values, so multiply by (max - min + 1) = 6, which gives 0..5.999..., and floor gives 0..5. Adding min shifts that to 1..6. Without the + 1 you would multiply by 5 and only ever produce 0..4, so the top value becomes impossible. Note this is not cryptographically secure; use crypto.getRandomValues for tokens.

questions.question:
What does this print, and what is the correct way to add one month?

const d = new Date(2026, 0, 31);
d.setMonth(d.getMonth() + 1);
console.log(d.toDateString());
console.log(new Date(2026, 1, 29).toDateString());

questions.answer:
It prints 'Tue Mar 03 2026' and 'Sun Mar 01 2026'.

Month 0 is January, so new Date(2026, 0, 31) is 31 January. setMonth(1) asks for 31 February, which does not exist, so the Date rolls over into March: 3 March 2026. The second line shows the same rollover: 2026 is not a leap year, so 29 February becomes 1 March. Date never throws on out-of-range parts; it normalises them.

Correct month addition clamps to the last day of the target month:

const addMonths = (date, n) => {
  const d = new Date(date);
  const day = d.getDate();
  d.setDate(1);
  d.setMonth(d.getMonth() + n);
  const lastDay = new Date(d.getFullYear(), d.getMonth() + 1, 0).getDate();
  d.setDate(Math.min(day, lastDay));
  return d;
};

console.log(addMonths(new Date(2026, 0, 31), 1).toDateString());
// Sat Feb 28 2026

