questions.answer:
const daysBetween = (a, b) => {
  const utcMidnight = (d) => Date.UTC(d.getFullYear(), d.getMonth(), d.getDate());
  return Math.round((utcMidnight(b) - utcMidnight(a)) / 86_400_000);
};

console.log(daysBetween(new Date(2026, 0, 1), new Date(2026, 0, 31))); // 30

const label = new Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-IN", {
  dateStyle: "medium",
  timeZone: "UTC",
}).format(new Date("2026-01-15T10:30:00Z"));
console.log(label); // 15 Jan 2026

Subtracting two Dates directly divides milliseconds that include time-of-day, so 1 Jan 23:00 to 2 Jan 01:00 would be 0.08 days. Flattening both to UTC midnight removes the clock time. Using Date.UTC (rather than local midnight) removes DST, because UTC has no 23/25-hour days. Math.round absorbs any residual offset.
